course: everyday
lesson: 45
title: Pools, lakes and ice
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Drowning is usually quick and silent: a child in trouble often cannot shout or wave, so someone must be watching swimmers all the time, even in a small pool.
explain: Around pools you walk, because wet tiles are slippery, and you go into water you do not know feet first, because a dive into shallow water can hit the bottom and badly hurt your head and neck.
explain: Lakes and rivers hide their dangers: the bottom may drop suddenly, the water can be very cold, and there may be weeds, rocks or currents you cannot see; on a boat you always wear a life jacket.
explain: Ice on a pond may look solid but can be thin, especially near streams, bridges and the edges; children go on ice only where an adult has checked it is thick enough, and in the UK and Ireland ice is almost never safe to walk on.
explain: If a friend falls through the ice, you do not go out to him: you call for help, then reach with a long branch or throw something that floats, while you stay on safe ground.
explain: A boy in Minnesota walked onto a frozen pond to fetch a ball; the ice cracked and he fell through into the freezing water, and firefighters pulled him out.
explain: A girl in a backyard pool in California got her long hair caught near a pool drain; an adult switched off the pump, and she was freed after a frightening moment.
fact: Drowning can happen quickly and quietly, often without any shouting or splashing.
fact: A grown-up should watch children in and near water all the time.
fact: Wet pool sides are slippery, so you walk, never run.
fact: Diving into shallow water can cause serious head and neck injuries.
fact: You enter water you do not know feet first.
fact: Lakes and rivers can be much colder and deeper than they look.
fact: Everyone on a boat should wear a life jacket.
fact: Ice is thinner near moving water, such as streams, inlets and under bridges.
fact: You never go on ice unless an adult has checked that it is safe.
fact: If someone falls through ice, you call for help and reach or throw something to them; you do not go out onto the ice. Only a trained rescuer goes into the water.
fact: If you fall through ice, you turn back the way you came, where the ice held you, kick and slide onto the ice on your tummy, then roll away; lying down spreads your weight so the ice is less likely to break.
fact: Sudden cold water makes you gasp and breathe fast, which makes swimming hard.
fact: Ice that looks solid can be thin, especially near the edges, over moving water and in spring.
fact: Falling into icy water makes you gasp and quickly takes away your strength.
exercise: Why must someone watch children in a pool all the time? | Drowning can happen quickly and without any noise | Lifeguards are only there to keep the pool clean | Children always shout loudly when in trouble | Pools are only dangerous at the deep end
exercise: Why should you walk, not run, around a swimming pool? | The wet floor is slippery and you could fall and hit your head | Running makes the water splash too much | Running scares the other swimmers | The lifeguard cannot count how many people are in the pool if you run about
exercise: Why is it dangerous to dive into water when you do not know how deep it is? | You could hit the bottom and hurt your head or neck | The water will be too cold for diving | Diving makes your ears hurt, but only in lakes and rivers | Fish may be swimming near the top
exercise: How should you enter a lake you have never swum in before? | Feet first, slowly | Head first, with a big dive | With a running jump | By sliding in on your back
exercise: Why can a lake be more dangerous than a pool? | It can be colder and deeper, with hidden rocks and weeds | Lake water is too thick to swim in | Lakes have no bottom in the middle | Lake water always makes you sink much faster than clean pool water
exercise: What must everyone wear on a small boat on a lake? | A life jacket | A swimming cap | A pair of goggles | Arm floats only for the youngest
exercise: Where is ice on a frozen pond most likely to be thin? | Near a stream flowing into it | In the middle, far from any stream | Where there is the most snow on top | Where children have skated before
exercise: When is it safe for a child to walk on a frozen pond? | Only when an adult has checked the ice is thick enough | Whenever it has been very cold for a whole day and night | When the ice is covered in snow | When other children are already on it
exercise: A friend falls through the ice on a pond. What should you do first? | Shout for help and call the emergency number | Run out onto the ice to grab him | Jump in to hold him up | Leave quickly so you do not get into trouble
exercise: How can you help a friend in icy water without going out onto the ice? | Hold out a long branch or throw something that floats | Lie down right next to the hole and hold both his hands | Walk out slowly to where he is | Throw heavy stones to break a path
exercise: A boy falls through the ice. Which way should he try to climb out? | Back the way he came, where the ice held him | Forward, the way he was going, as the ice ahead is new | Into the middle of the pond | Whichever way the wind blows
exercise: After getting onto the ice, why should you roll away instead of standing up? | Lying down spreads your weight so the ice is less likely to break | Standing makes you colder | Rolling makes you dry faster | Standing up on ice is against the rules, so you would get into trouble with the park keeper
exercise: Why does falling into very cold water make swimming hard? | The shock makes you gasp and breathe very fast | Cold water is heavier, so it pushes you down to the bottom | Cold water freezes your arms at once | Your clothes become lighter and float away
exercise: What is the safest choice in Ireland on a frosty morning by a frozen pond? | Stay off the ice, since it is rarely thick enough | Test it by jumping hard on the edge to see if it cracks | Walk on it if it looks white | Slide on it if a friend goes first
test: A girl in Minnesota sees older children skating on a lake near where a river flows in. What should she think? | The ice near the river is likely to be thin and dangerous | The ice must be safe everywhere if they are skating on it | River water makes ice thicker | Skating spreads their weight so any ice is safe
test: At a hotel pool in Florida, a boy wants to dive in at the shallow end because it looks deep enough. What is the safe choice? | Go in feet first, and dive only where the water is known to be deep | Dive in with his arms out in front | Dive in only if no one is looking | Jump in head first instead of diving, since jumping is always safe in a hotel pool
test: A dad looks at his phone for a few minutes while his children swim. Why is this risky? | A child can slip under quietly and he would not notice | His children are sure to splash him | The phone could fall in the pool | Children only get into trouble when he is watching them closely
test: Which is true about someone who is drowning? | They often cannot shout or wave for help | They always shout and splash loudly | They float on their back while drowning | They can always hold on to the side
test: A dog runs onto a frozen pond and falls through. What should its owner's daughter do? | Stay off the ice, call for help and call to the dog from the bank | Run onto the ice to pull it out | Slide out on her tummy to grab its collar | Throw herself into the water to help the dog swim back to the bank
test: A boy in Canada falls through the ice near the bank. How should he get out? | Arms on the ice behind him, kick his legs, slide out and roll away | Stand up and climb out as fast as he can | Swim under the ice towards the middle, where it is thicker and safer to climb out | Stay very still and wait for spring
test: Three friends go fishing in a small boat on a lake on a calm day. What is the most important rule? | All of them wear life jackets the whole time | Only the youngest needs a life jacket | No life jackets are needed when it is calm | Life jackets are only for people who cannot swim
test: Why might a boy who swims well in a warm pool get into trouble in a cold mountain lake? | The cold water can shock him and tire him quickly | Lake water does not hold up swimmers the way pool water does | Mountain lakes have no bottom | He cannot see under the water
test: A girl wants to rescue a friend in water. Which is the safest order? | Call for help, reach or throw something, go in only if trained | Jump in first, then call for help | Go in, then reach, then throw | Throw herself in to hold her friend up until somebody else comes along to help
test: pond's frozen, can we go on it | Not unless an adult has checked it | Yes, ice is always thick | Yes, near the stream is best | Yes, if it looks white
test: friend fell through the ice! | Call for help and throw something, stay off | Run out across the ice and grab him fast | Jump in too | Walk out slowly to him
test: i fell through the ice, how do i get out | Go back the way you came, kick and slide on your tummy | Stand up and climb | Swim to the middle | Wait without moving, someone will come and get you out
test: shallow end, can i dive | No, diving into shallow water can hurt your neck | Yes, if you go fast | Yes, head first is safest | Yes, if your eyes are open and you dive really flat
test: lake's frozen, looks solid, can we slide on it | No, stay off, ice can crack and you fall through | Yes, if it looks white | Yes, if you stay near the edge | Yes, if you go one at a time
test: mate fell through the ice!! | Shout for help and reach a stick from the bank | Run onto the ice to grab him, fast as you can | Jump in after him | Stamp on the ice to break a path
test: Jack says pond ice is always thickest at the edges. true? | No, edges are often thin and weak | Yes, edges freeze first | Yes, the middle always melts | Yes, unless it is snowing
test: did u hear a lad went through the ice on the pond near the bridge, his mates had to pull him out with a branch | Real lesson: ice is thinner near moving water, so never go on ice an adult has not checked | Real lesson: branches are the best way onto ice | It proves only ponds freeze thinly | It is gossip, so ice is always safe in winter as long as it is really cold out
test: my cousin reckons it's fine to dive in the shallow end if u dive flat, he does it all the time | Not safe; diving into shallow water can hurt your head and neck badly | He is right, a flat dive is always safe | He is right, if the water is warm | He is right, as long as a friend watches from the side to check the water is deep enough
test: Hugo slipped running round the pool and cut his chin, people say he's a show-off, pass it on | Do not pass it on; he got hurt, and the lesson is to walk on wet pool sides | Pass it on, because show-offs deserve it | Pass it on, so he stops coming to the pool | Pass it on, and say he needed ten stitches and cried in front of the lifeguards
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the thickness of ice near streams, inlets and bridges? | The ice is thinner where water moves beneath it | The ice is thicker where water moves beneath it | The ice is of uniform thickness everywhere | The ice is thickest beneath bridges because of shade
exam: Identify the principal hazard of diving into shallow water. | Serious injury to the head and neck | A mild chill of the skin | The loss of a swimming cap | Water entering the ears and causing pain
exam: It being given that children are in or near water, select the arrangement consistent with established guidance. | A grown-up must watch them at all times | A grown-up need watch only when they are in deep water | Older children may supervise younger ones alone | No supervision is required in a shallow pool
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning lakes and rivers. | They may be much colder and deeper than they appear | They are always warmer than the sea | Their depth may be judged reliably by eye | They contain no currents of any kind
